      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "Label": "UNLABELLED",
          "content": "Complications after endoscopic sinus surgery are rare and occur in about 0.5 % of all cases. However postoperative symptoms such as severe cephalgia and nasal discharge of clear fluids indicate a cerebrospinal fluid leakage."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A 64 year-old-patient presented to our clinic four months after an endoscopic sinus surgery complaining of the two above mentioned symptoms for about 4 weeks. In a computed tomography study of the frontobasis there was no evidence of an osseous defect. An MRI of the head revealed a bilateral temporal fluid collection as seen in bilateral chronic subdural hematoma. Therefore the present illness was no consequence of the preceding surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Significant symptoms of severe postoperative complications after endoscopic sinus surgery can still be misleading. Therefore accurate diagnostic and imaging procedures are always crucial to support the initial diagnosis."
        }
      ]},
